/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"production-level-3","number":3,"title":"Module 3 — Session","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"Ableton classroom (Manual ch. 7 opening): Arrangement is the traditional timeline. Session exists because live order, theatre cues, and improvisation-before-product are poorly served by a ruler. Clips play in any order. The grid is random a","modules":[{"id":"production-m-3-1","code":"3","title":"Session as a song instrument","blurb":"Ableton classroom (Manual ch. 7 opening): Arrangement is the traditional timeline. Session exists because live order, theatre cues, and improvisation-before-product are poorly served by a ruler. Clips play in any order. The grid is random a","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-6","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 3 — Session as a song instrument","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Treat Session View as the place you <em>write</em> Deliverable 1 — not a sketchpad you later dump onto a ruler.   <strong>Required.</strong> Gate <strong>B</strong> sits after 2.4.   <strong>Do not:</strong> Polish a Session→Arrangement print as Deliverable 2. Do not invent BPMs. Do not open a synthesizer course. Operator / Max for Live stay closed.   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Live 12 Standard Manual ch. 7 (Session), ch. 16 / 19 (launch + Capture MIDI); Ableton KB <em>Capture MIDI</em>; songwriting KB §7.2, §8.7, §9.2–§9.5; Launchkey MK4 Clip / Drum modes (0.10); Sound on Sound / Music City SF (Session as improvisation-before-product — named as <em>document</em>, not capstone)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton classroom (Manual ch. 7 opening): Arrangement is the traditional timeline. Session exists because live order, theatre cues, and improvisation-before-product are poorly served by a ruler. Clips play <strong>in any order</strong>. The grid is <strong>random access</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sound on Sound’s association — capturing ideas, jamming, performing, experimenting — is this section’s <em>mindset</em>. You will still <strong>finish</strong> here on purpose. Finishing is a decision, not a view."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 3.1 — Clips, scenes, form","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Scene = section. Clip = ingredient. Build a named scene list that could be a song. Use silence as composition."},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.2 is the recommended first method for this studio. You will follow its <em>shape</em> (scenes as sections, launch like a band) and refuse its last two steps as a <em>capstone</em> (Arrangement Record + polish on the ruler is <strong>documentation</strong>, Section 3.3)."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-1","title":"3.1.1 — The Session grid is a song you have not ordered yet","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.1 — The Session grid is a song you have not ordered yet","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The Session grid is a song you have not ordered yet.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.1: clips can be played at any time and in any order. Layout does <strong>not</strong> predetermine order. That is the opposite of a tape machine."},{"type":"prose","html":"A song, for this course, is <strong>named sections you can launch</strong>. The grid holds the ingredients and the cues. The <em>order</em> is what you perform (2.3) or later write linearly (Section B — a <strong>different</strong> song)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Loop-trap reminder (0.3): eight looping bars in one slot is not a song. Six named scenes with almost nothing in them is closer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can point at empty rows and say which sections they will be."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-2","title":"3.1.2 — Clip = ingredient (one clip per track at a time)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.2 — Clip = ingredient (one clip per track at a time)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A track plays one clip. Variations of the same part live in one column.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.2: each <strong>vertical</strong> column (track) plays <strong>only one clip at a time</strong>. So variations of the same part belong in the <strong>same column</strong>: verse drums / chorus drums / breakdown drums."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you put verse drums and chorus drums on two tracks and launch both scenes carelessly, you get two kits. That is a mix problem you invented with architecture."},{"type":"prose","html":"Group-track slots show shading when a child has a clip in that scene, plus a group launch (7.1). Useful later. This week: one instrument family per column."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pressing <strong>0</strong> with a Session clip selected deactivates that clip (7.1). Deactivate ≠ stop. Know which you meant."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Verse and chorus versions of the <em>same</em> part sit in one column, two rows."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-3","title":"3.1.3 — Scene = section (launch the band)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.3 — Scene = section (launch the band)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A row is a section. Launch it like a band cue.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.2: horizontal rows are <strong>scenes</strong>. The Scene Launch button lives in the <strong>Main</strong> track (rightmost column). One click launches every clip in that row."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is a band cue: “we are in the chorus now.” Not “please start the snare track.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"After a launch, the scene <strong>below</strong> is selected as next unless <strong>Select Next Scene on Launch</strong> is Off (Launch Settings). Top-to-bottom triggering is the default DJ/band habit."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cancel a triggered-but-not-yet-playing scene from the Main track context menu (<strong>Cancel Scene Launch</strong>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One Scene Launch starts at least two tracks together."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-4","title":"3.1.4 — Name scenes like an album, not","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.4 — Name scenes like an album, not","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Intro, Verse, Chorus — words you can still read in a month.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.2: scenes can be renamed; Tab walks you to the next scene while renaming. Color palette is in the same context menu."},{"type":"prose","html":"Required starter list (pop default from 0.4–0.8 / KB §8.7). Club labs may swap the nouns later; the <em>habit</em> does not change:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Intro</code> · <code>Verse</code> · <code>Pre</code> · <code>Chorus</code> · <code>Bridge</code> · <code>Outro</code>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Forbidden: <code>scene 3</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>asdf</code>, <code>copy</code>. Beginner-mistake canon #4."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scene <strong>numbers</strong> on the right are position, not names. They change when you drag. Do not memorize numbers."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Practice (section):</strong> Six empty named scenes before you record a note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> A stranger could launch your song from the names alone."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-5","title":"3.1.5 — Stop buttons and silence as composition","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.5 — Stop buttons and silence as composition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Stop buttons and silence as composition.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Each slot can hold a clip <strong>or</strong> a <strong>Clip Stop</strong> button. Clicking stop in a track stops that track’s Session clip (7.1). A scene that contains stops will <strong>silence</strong> those tracks when launched."},{"type":"prose","html":"Silence is a section. A breakdown that kills the bass is a stop (or an empty slot with a stop), not a new bass patch at −∞ dB."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Control Bar Play button can stay lit after a Session clip stops — Arrangement Position <strong>keeps running</strong> (7.1). That clock is how Live knows where to print if you record into Arrangement. Double-Stop returns the song position to 1.1.1 and stops the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can launch a scene that is <em>mostly</em> silence on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-6","title":"3.1.6 — Removing stop buttons so a scene does not kill a bed","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.6 — Removing stop buttons so a scene does not kill a bed","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Removing stop buttons so a scene does not kill a bed.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.4.2: <strong>Edit → Add/Remove Stop Button</strong>. If scene 3 must not touch track 4 (a pad bed that should ring under verse <em>and</em> chorus), remove the stop in that slot."},{"type":"prose","html":"A slot with <strong>no clip and no stop</strong> is a “leave it” cell. The track keeps doing whatever it was doing."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is how a drone, a vinyl crackle, or a converted topline (1.5) survives a scene change without being re-triggered."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One bed track plays through two named scenes because you removed the stop."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-7","title":"3.1.7 — Select on Launch — when to turn it off","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.7 — Select on Launch — when to turn it off","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> When to turn it off.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.4.1: by default, launching a clip also <strong>selects</strong> it so Clip View follows. Power-user problem: you were editing a return-track device; launching a clip steals focus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Launch Settings → Select on Launch → Off</strong> when you are mixing or mapping and only want the music to change."},{"type":"prose","html":"For <em>writing</em> week, leave it <strong>on</strong> so you see what you just launched. Turn it off on purpose, notice the difference, turn it back on."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You have toggled the preference once and know which state you are in."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-8","title":"3.1.8 — Scene tempo and meter — use only on purpose","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.8 — Scene tempo and meter — use only on purpose","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Use only on purpose.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.2.1: drag the left edge of the Main track header to show <strong>Scene Tempo</strong> and <strong>Scene Time Signature</strong>. Launching that scene sets the project to those values. Legal range: tempo 20–999 BPM; meter numerator 1–99; denominator 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16."},{"type":"prose","html":"Colored Scene Launch buttons mark scenes that carry tempo or meter changes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This course default:</strong> leave them empty. One Set tempo, one meter, unless you can say why (half-time <em>feel</em> is not a tempo change — 1.3.5). A scene that secretly drops to another BPM is a foot-gun when you later print to Arrangement."},{"type":"prose","html":"Disable / reset: context menu <strong>Return to Default</strong>, or Delete, or double-click the control."},{"type":"prose","html":"Old Live Sets that hid tempo in the <em>scene name</em> get those values migrated to the controls when opened in Live 11+."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> All six named scenes have tempo/meter fields empty (or you wrote the reason they are not)."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-9","title":"3.1.9 — Follow Actions: glue, not a substitute for form","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.9 — Follow Actions: glue, not a substitute for form","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Follow Actions: glue, not a substitute for form.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Clip Follow Actions (and scene Follow Actions in Scene View, 7.2.2) can start the next clip or scene when one finishes. They are <strong>glue</strong>: a fill that always leads into the chorus clip; a one-shot that then plays the loop."},{"type":"prose","html":"They are <strong>not</strong> a song. A chain of Follow Actions that “plays the album” while you leave the room is a sequencer you hid. You still need named sections and a contrast decision (2.4)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use one Follow Action this week if you want: a one-bar fill clip that then plays the chorus loop. Then launch scenes with your hands."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can disable every Follow Action and still perform the form."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-1-10","title":"3.1.10 — Capture and Insert Scene as a writing tool","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.10 — Capture and Insert Scene as a writing tool","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Capture and Insert Scene as a writing tool.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.4.3 (<strong>Create</strong> menu):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Insert Scene</strong> — empty row below the selection.  ","<strong>Capture and Insert Scene</strong> — new scene below, <strong>copies of every currently running clip</strong>, launches the new scene immediately with no gap."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"That is how you freeze a lucky combination: verse drums + chorus bass you accidentally left running + a hummed MIDI clip. Name the new scene <em>before</em> you forget why it was good."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is different from <strong>Capture MIDI</strong> (2.2.3), which retrieves notes you played. This command photographs the <em>grid state</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 3.1 practice:</strong> Six empty named scenes (Intro / Verse / Pre / Chorus / Bridge / Outro or club equivalents).   <strong>Section 3.1 checkpoint:</strong> You can launch silence on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-1","title":"3.2.1 — Three starting doors in Session","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.1 — Three starting doors in Session","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Three starting doors in Session (groove / harmony / topline).  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Same table as 1.5.1 / KB §9.1, now as <em>Session architecture</em>:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Door","First clip you put in <code>Verse</code>","Next clip, same scene"],"rows":[["Groove","Drum Rack loop","Bass that locks with kick"],["Harmony","Chord clip","Bass, then a rhythm"],["Topline","Hum-converted MIDI (1.5.6) or a played lead","Harmony under it"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick <strong>one</strong> door for Deliverable 1. Do not start three songs in one Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The first non-empty clip in <code>Verse</code> matches the door you wrote down."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-2","title":"3.2.2 — Record into a Session slot","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.2 — Record into a Session slot","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Record into a Session slot.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Arm the MIDI (or audio) track. Session Record in the Control Bar (or MK4 Record — hardware Record follows the <strong>active view</strong>, KB §5.5: Session → Session Record). Click a slot’s record, or record into the selected empty slot."},{"type":"prose","html":"You are capturing a <strong>clip</strong>, not a song. Keep it 4–8 bars unless you have a reason. Name it immediately (<code>DR_verse</code>, <code>BS_verse</code>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Audio recording uses the same slot logic. Hygiene (0.10): you are already in a Project folder."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One new clip appeared in a <em>named</em> scene, not in row 1 by accident."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-3","title":"3.2.3 — Capture MIDI after the fact","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.3 — Capture MIDI after the fact","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Capture MIDI after the fact.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton KB <em>Capture MIDI</em> + Manual 19.10:"},{"type":"prose","html":"Live is <strong>always listening</strong> on <strong>armed</strong> or <strong>input-monitored (In)</strong> MIDI tracks. If you played something good and were not recording, press <strong>Capture MIDI</strong> on the transport. Live creates a new MIDI clip with what you just played, on every armed/monitored track that has data."},{"type":"list","items":["Button <strong>grey</strong> = nothing to catch. <strong>Black</strong> = data waiting.  ","Empty Set, playback <strong>off</strong>, no clips yet: Capture may <strong>set the tempo</strong> from what you played and start playback. If the Set <strong>already has clips</strong> or is <strong>playing</strong>, tempo is left alone.  ","Buffer: when <strong>16384</strong> events fill, the oldest <strong>1024</strong> are discarded. Play forever and the beginning evaporates.  ","CPU cost is negligible (MIDI is tiny).  ","Hardware synth notes you hear from the <em>synth’s</em> speakers are <strong>not</strong> captured unless MIDI actually reaches Live (USB/DIN, Track enabled, arm or In)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture is the “I had it” button from 0.10.7. Four bars of nonsense is still legal."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Play without recording. Capture. Find the clip in a Session slot."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-4","title":"3.2.4 — Layer-by-layer clips (not one frozen “print”)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.4 — Layer-by-layer clips (not one frozen “print”)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Layer-by-layer clips (not one frozen “print”).  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.2 steps 2–5, Session-native:"},{"type":"list","items":["Tempo and approximate key (you already chose; do not invent a new BPM).  ","Scene <code>Verse</code>: 4–8 bar drums.  ","Same row: bass that locks with kick.  ","Same row: chords.  ","Duplicate the scene → thin it for verse / fill it for chorus (2.4)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Each layer is its <strong>own clip on its own track</strong>. A stereo bounce of “the loop” is a dead end: you cannot mute the bass in the bridge."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Drums, bass, and chords are three clips, one scene."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-5","title":"3.2.5 — MK4 Drum mode + keys in one Session","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.5 — MK4 Drum mode + keys in one Session","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> MK4 Drum mode + keys in one Session.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"You need both surfaces in one song:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Drum mode</strong> (Shift + Pad 10): notes 36–51, channel 10 → Drum Rack / sliced phrase (1.4).  ","<strong>Keys</strong>: the 61-key bed → bass, chords, topline.  ","<strong>Clip mode</strong> (Shift + Pad 9): launch the scenes you named."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not hunt a synth while the pads are still in Clip mode (0.10.8). Do not enable MPE on the MK4 port."},{"type":"prose","html":"Arm the track you mean. Capture or record. Switch mode. The modes are layers, not different USB devices."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> In one sitting you recorded a pad pattern <em>and</em> a keys clip without replugging anything."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-6","title":"3.2.6 — Quantize after — strength is a feel choice","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.6 — Quantize after — strength is a feel choice","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Strength is a feel choice.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.5: quantize <strong>lightly</strong>, not always 100%. Record first (or Capture first). Then clip-quantize or a MIDI Transform Quantize."},{"type":"prose","html":"100% to the grid is a <strong>sound</strong>. 60–80% is often the pocket. 0% is legal if the groove is the point (1.3 unwarped cousin)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Record Quantization (Edit menu) quantizes <em>as you record</em>. Leave it <strong>off</strong> this week so you can hear what your hands did, then quantize on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One clip exists in two versions: raw Capture, and a quantized duplicate. You kept the one that feels like the section."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-7","title":"3.2.7 — Duplicate to make Verse_B","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.7 — Duplicate to make Verse_B","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Duplicate to make Verse_B.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate the <code>Verse</code> scene (or duplicate clips downward). Rename <code>Verse_B</code>. Change <strong>one</strong> thing: a hat pattern, a bass rest, a chord inversion."},{"type":"prose","html":"You now have variation without a new idea. Follow Actions are allowed to bounce A/B as glue (2.1.9). Naming is still required."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> <code>Verse</code> and <code>Verse_B</code> are not identical, and both names are readable."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-8","title":"3.2.8 — Empty clip as a placeholder so the scene still means something","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.8 — Empty clip as a placeholder so the scene still means something","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Empty clip as a placeholder so the scene still means something.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Double-click an empty MIDI slot, or <strong>Ctrl+Shift+M</strong> (Manual 10.3), to insert an <strong>empty MIDI clip</strong>. It launches, plays nothing, and keeps the scene’s <em>shape</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it when the chorus will have a lead you have not written. The row still says <code>Chorus</code>. You will not “forget a column.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"An empty clip is different from a removed stop (2.1.6). Empty clip = this track <em>has a part here</em>, currently rest. No stop = this track <em>continues</em> from the previous scene."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> <code>Chorus</code> has at least one empty placeholder on a track that will matter later."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-9","title":"3.2.9 — Velocity as section contrast","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.9 — Velocity as section contrast","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Velocity as section contrast.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.5 / §9.4: soft verse, harder chorus. Same notes, different velocity, is a legal lever (2.4 will demand two levers). You do not need a new patch."},{"type":"prose","html":"Select the chorus clip’s notes, raise velocity. Or play it again harder and keep that take."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Verse and chorus clips of the same part have visibly different velocity."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-2-10","title":"3.2.10 — Default Core Library sounds are legal finishers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.10 — Default Core Library sounds are legal finishers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Default Core Library sounds are legal finishers.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §1.2 principle 3 and 0.10.10 again, because Session week is when people open Vital."},{"type":"prose","html":"Core Library drums, a stock bass, a stock keys patch. You may finish Deliverable 1 on these. Sound Design is another course. Operator is installed and <strong>not</strong> this homework."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 3.2 checkpoint:</strong> Three layered clips in one named scene, stock sounds, Capture or record used on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-1","title":"3.3.1 — 2×8 focus ring and scene launch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.1 — 2×8 focus ring and scene launch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> 2×8 focus ring and scene launch.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"MK4 Clip mode: the 16 pads are a <strong>2×8 window</strong> onto the Session grid (focus ring). They do not show the whole song. Scene launch (scene buttons / dedicated scene controls per Novation DAW map) moves you through <strong>rows</strong>. Arrow / scene nav moves the ring."},{"type":"prose","html":"You prepared six scenes. The ring may only show two rows of eight tracks. Navigate on purpose. If a launch “does nothing,” you are looking at empty cells or the wrong mode — not a broken Live."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can move the focus ring until your named scenes are the ones the pads address."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-2","title":"3.3.2 — Clip vs scene vs clip-stop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.2 — Clip vs scene vs clip-stop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Clip vs scene vs clip-stop.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Three different buttons, three different sentences:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Control","Means"],"rows":[["<strong>Clip launch</strong>","This track, this ingredient, now"],["<strong>Scene launch</strong>","This whole section (the band cue)"],["<strong>Clip stop</strong>","This track, silence (or return toward Arrangement if that track was following the ruler)"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Launching a Session clip <strong>stops that track’s Arrangement</strong> (Manual 7.5). Stopping a Session clip on a track that had Arrangement material leaves <strong>silence</strong> until <strong>Back to Arrangement</strong>. Learn the orange button in 2.3.6 before you panic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can demonstrate all three without looking at a cheat sheet."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-3","title":"3.3.3 — 90-second radio pass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.3 — 90-second radio pass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> 90-second radio pass.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Perform the six scenes in an order that could be a short radio edit. About <strong>90 seconds</strong> is enough to prove you can leave a loop. Use the MK4 if it is plugged in; mouse is legal (2.3.8)."},{"type":"prose","html":"You are not mixing. You are proving <strong>form</strong>. Count: can a listener hear verse versus chorus?"},{"type":"prose","html":"If you cannot last 90 seconds without getting lost, the scenes are unnamed or the stops are wrong — go back to 2.1."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One pass, start to stop, without touching the mouse if the MK4 is present."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-4","title":"3.3.4 — Record Session into Arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.4 — Record Session into Arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Record Session into Arrangement.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.5: Arrangement Record <strong>logs</strong> what you do:"},{"type":"list","items":["Which clips launched  ","Clip-property changes  ","Mixer / device moves (automation)  ","Tempo and meter changes <strong>if they live in the launched scenes</strong>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"It does <strong>not</strong> create new audio (unless you were also recording input). It copies Session clips onto the ruler at the times you launched them."},{"type":"prose","html":"Stop recording. Open Arrangement. You should see blocks that match the pass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The ruler contains a document of the 90-second pass, on the correct tracks."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-5","title":"3.3.5 — Independent clips: why this print is not Deliverable 2","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.5 — Independent clips: why this print is not Deliverable 2","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Session clips and Arrangement clips are separate. A recorded jam is an archive, not song 2.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 7.5, last law: clips in Session and Arrangement <strong>exist independently</strong>. You can jam onto the ruler again and again. You can even copy Arrangement material <em>back</em> to Session (paste preserves top-to-bottom time; <strong>Consolidate Time to New Scene</strong> makes one new scene from a time selection)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Course lock:</strong> that independence is why dumping Session into Arrangement <strong>fails Gate E</strong>. Deliverable 2 is a <strong>different</strong> song, written on the timeline (locators, comps, overdubs — Modules 8–11). This print is an archive of Deliverable 1’s performance, or a sketch. Beginner-mistake canon #5."},{"type":"prose","html":"Industry “sketch then polish on the timeline” is a real workflow. It is not <em>this</em> school’s two-song rule."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can say which song this print belongs to (song 1’s archive) and which song it must not become."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-6","title":"3.3.6 — Back to Arrangement — what the orange button is telling you","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.6 — Back to Arrangement — what the orange button is telling you","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> What the orange button is telling you.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"After any Session launch, Live is <strong>no longer</strong> playing that track’s Arrangement. The <strong>Back to Arrangement</strong> button lights (Session and Arrangement views). Press it to force every track back to the ruler."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Stop All Clips</strong> (Main track status) disables Arrangement clips too — different button, different sentence."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you hear your jam but the Arrangement looks full, you are listening to Session. Orange means “what you hear ≠ the Arrangement.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can make the orange button appear and disappear on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-7","title":"3.3.7 — Export a listen file from the performance","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.7 — Export a listen file from the performance","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Export a listen file from the performance.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Export Audio/Video from the <strong>Arrangement document</strong> of the pass, or record a bounce of the Session performance. A listen file is for <em>you</em> and for later Gate C. It is not a master."},{"type":"prose","html":"Name it <code>D1_pass_YYYYMMDD.wav</code> inside the Project folder. Collect All and Save."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The wav exists on disk and plays the 90-second form."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-3-8","title":"3.3.8 — Mouse / computer-keyboard alternate if hardware is away","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.8 — Mouse / computer-keyboard alternate if hardware is away","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Mouse / computer-keyboard alternate if hardware is away.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Gate B allows MK4 <strong>or</strong> mouse. Manual 7.1: clip launch by triangle or by selecting + Enter; arrow keys move; scenes from the Main track; computer keyboard or MIDI can be mapped."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the Launchkey is in a bag: you still do the 90-second pass. Do not skip the section. When the MK4 returns, repeat once so Clip mode is in the hands."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 3.3 mustNotCover:</strong> polishing this print as the Arrangement capstone."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 3.3 checkpoint:</strong> A listen file exists; you know it is not song 2."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-1","title":"3.4.1 — Contrast checklist — at least two levers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.1 — Contrast checklist — at least two levers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> At least two levers.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.4 — change <strong>at least two</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] More / fewer instruments  ","[ ] Higher melodic range  ","[ ] Brighter EQ / open hi-hats  ","[ ] Wider stereo (<strong>carefully</strong>)  ","[ ] Harmonic lift (e.g. add IV or a secondary dominant later)  ","[ ] Drum fill into the downbeat of the chorus  ","[ ] Bass rhythm denser or more locked  "],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Two. Not seven. A louder fader alone is <strong>not</strong> a chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write the two levers in the scene info text (<code>Chorus — hats + extra clip</code>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The <code>Chorus</code> scene info names two levers."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-2","title":"3.4.2 — Subtract to make a verse","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.2 — Subtract to make a verse","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Subtract to make a verse.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate <code>Chorus</code> downward, rename <code>Verse</code> if you wrote chorus first (groove-door people often do). <strong>Remove</strong> clips or replace them with sparser ones. EDMProd subtractive arrangement and Beat Kitchen add/subtract are the same family (0.3, 0.9)."},{"type":"prose","html":"A verse with everything muted except kick and a topline is legal. A verse that is the chorus at −3 dB is not two levers."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> <code>Verse</code> has fewer ingredients than <code>Chorus</code>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-3","title":"3.4.3 — Add to make a chorus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.3 — Add to make a chorus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Add to make a chorus.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"The other direction: <code>Verse</code> is the home. Duplicate. Add a hat clip, a doubled topline, a clap. Stock sounds (2.2.10)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Adding a new <strong>Vital patch</strong> is the wrong course. Adding a <strong>clip</strong> is this course."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can solo the clips that exist only in <code>Chorus</code>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-4","title":"3.4.4 — Register lift without a new patch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.4 — Register lift without a new patch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Register lift without a new patch.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.5: duplicate a clip, transpose <strong>+3 or +5</strong> as a lift idea. Same instrument, higher range. That is lever 2 (melodic range) without sound design."},{"type":"prose","html":"If it clashes, you learned something about the harmony door. Undo is cheap."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Chorus topline or chords sit higher than verse, same device."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-5","title":"3.4.5 — Fill into the downbeat as a scene edge","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.5 — Fill into the downbeat as a scene edge","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Fill into the downbeat as a scene edge.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A 1- or 2-bar fill clip at the <strong>end</strong> of <code>Pre</code> or <code>Verse</code>, Follow Action → <code>Chorus</code>, or you launch it by hand a bar early."},{"type":"prose","html":"The fill is a <strong>doorway</strong>, not a new section name. Beat Kitchen / pop form: the downbeat of the chorus is a contract. Arrive on time."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> There is a named fill clip that only makes sense as an edge."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-6","title":"3.4.6 — Mute as composition","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.6 — Mute as composition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Mute as composition.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Track mute, clip deactivate (0), or a scene of stops (2.1.5) — all legal subtractive writing. KB mute-as-contrast."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not confuse mixer mute (arrangement) with “I deleted the part.” Prefer deactivate or a sparser clip so you can bring it back."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One section is defined by what is <em>missing</em>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-7","title":"3.4.7 — KB energy percentages applied to your six scenes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.7 — KB energy percentages applied to your six scenes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> KB energy percentages applied to <em>your</em> six scenes.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §8.7 example map (pop — not a law of physics):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>Intro 50% → Verse 55% → Pre 70% → Chorus 90% → Verse 2 60% → Chorus 95% → Bridge 40–70% → Final Chorus 100% → Outro</code>"},{"type":"prose","html":"You have six scenes. Assign a <strong>guessed</strong> percentage to each by <em>listening</em>, then ask whether the numbers go up into <code>Chorus</code> and down into <code>Bridge</code>. If <code>Verse</code> and <code>Chorus</code> both feel like 90, you do not have two levers yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"Club labs will swap this map for 8-bar energy cells (0.9). Pop default stays until you skip into a lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Written % next to each of the six names. Chorus is not equal to verse."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l2-4-8","title":"3.4.8 — Launch the contrast; write what actually changed","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.8 — Launch the contrast; write what actually changed","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Launch the contrast; write what actually changed.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Perform <code>Verse</code> → <code>Chorus</code> (MK4 or mouse). Write <strong>two sentences</strong>: which levers fired, and whether a stranger would hear a chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you cannot hear it, change clips, not the master fader."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 3.4 practice:</strong> Two-lever contrast on the six-scene grid.   <strong>Checkpoint — Gate B</strong> (self-assessment, with 2.1–2.4):"},{"type":"table","headers":["#","Pass means"],"rows":[["1","<strong>Six named</strong> scenes exist (not <code>scene 3</code>)"],["2","You can launch them from the <strong>MK4 or the mouse</strong>"],["3","Verse vs chorus changes by <strong>at least two</strong> KB §9.4 levers"],["4","A 90-second pass (or longer) has a listen file that is <strong>not</strong> submitted as song 2"],["5","MPE still off; stock sounds still legal"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Module 0 had no gate. Gate A was warp / chop / law. Gate B is <strong>form you can launch</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Module 2 (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Live 12 Standard Manual, ch. 7 (clips, scenes, stop buttons, Select on Launch, Capture and Insert Scene, independent Session/Arrangement clips, Back to Arrangement, Consolidate Time to New Scene)  ","Ableton Live 12 Standard Manual, ch. 10.3 (empty MIDI clip); ch. 19.10 (Capture MIDI)  ","Ableton KB, <em>Capture MIDI</em> (arm or In; tempo rules; 16384-event buffer; grey/black button)  ","Songwriting KB §8.7 (section jobs + energy % example), §9.1–§9.5 (doors, Session method, contrast levers, MIDI habits)  ","Launchkey MK4 / KB §5.4–5.5 (Clip / Drum / Record-follows-view)  ","EDMProd subtractive arrangement + Beat Kitchen add/subtract (already taught in 0.3 / 0.9; applied here)  ","Syllabus-named: Sound on Sound Session-as-improv; Music City SF / Soundfly (document ≠ capstone)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Next:</strong> Modules 3–6 are <strong>skippable</strong> Session genre labs. Module 7 is Capstone C (Deliverable 1). Skip the labs and go to 7 when Gate B is honest."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
